   148	\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{\const{0.78}\linewidth}@{}}\toprule
   149	Question & Rule \\\midrule
   150	Q1 & $d = g_{C^*} - g_{F_{111}}$, pooled, for D2+H3 and X7-SC. Direction: Holm's procedure~\cite{holm1979simple} on calibrated two-sided $p$-values for $d = \const{0}$. Size: the calibrated Bonferroni interval $I$ for $d$. Categories, in order: \emph{materially lower} if $I$ lies above $\const{0.05}$ (R3 lower) or below $\const{-0.05}$ (C$^*$ lower); \emph{equivalent} if $I$ lies within $[\const{-0.05}, \const{0.05}]$, with a direction label when Holm rejects $d = \const{0}$; \emph{ordered, size not established} if Holm rejects and neither applies; otherwise \emph{inconclusive}. A rule is called better only with the first category in its favour and a paired gross-efficiency difference (that rule minus the other) whose nominal one-sided \const{97.5}\% lower bound is at least $\const{-0.01}$ (descriptive; no calibrated joint guarantee). If that lower bound is below $\const{-0.01}$, the gain category stands and the result is labelled ``efficiency non-inferiority unresolved''; it is labelled ``lower gain with an efficiency loss'' only if the corresponding upper bound is below $\const{-0.01}$. \\
