6        The attacks we test against
Every unilateral attacker family reads only the provider’s own history across its identities, the round,
its own parameters, and the rule’s public constants T and L; it does not observe the remaining global
budget or other identities’ ranks. Coalition members may share their members’ own histories. For
each parametrised family a registered search (CMA-ES with restarts on training seeds) finds the
attack. A rule’s truthful reference is then selected on training seeds from one menu built from
its D2+H3 and X7-SC searches and frozen; the W1 welfare searches, which need that reference,
run after the freeze; coalitions use their own staged joint truthful reference; and no evaluation-seed
episode of a market runs before all of its choices are frozen.
     H4’s code was committed only by its hash before the lock and released after the report review;
it is in sealed2/h4/ of the release (bundle SHA-256 cba512aa...), with its author’s note on which
families it contains as special cases.

                                                      8
Search quality. Scheduled cells get a doubling check: the registered search continues with as
many restarts again, and the cell is flagged if the doubled search improves the attacker’s normalised
profit or the reference’s normalised profit (profit cells), the welfare attack’s efficiency (welfare cells),
or the coalition objective by at least 0.005, or if a W1 search finds a feasible candidate where the
original found none. A doubled search’s winner is reported beside the cell and never replaces its
frozen estimate. Cells without a scheduled check carry no search-quality claim. A cell is missing if
it did not complete or is invalid: not run (a cap stop or a cut), a refused sample, a nonfinite statistic,
a search with no finite candidate, or a failed load of H4; a missing cell removes every decision that
uses it. A search-flagged cell keeps its decision, labelled as a decision about the prescribed search
procedure, and leaves that rule’s robustness statement for that family unanswered. The search
bounds exposure to the searched families and optimiser; it does not certify robustness.


7    The second test: design and registration
The first test. The first test (October 2026) locked its claim before R3 was implemented. The
original claim failed because neither uniform-audit baseline met its criterion against the sealed
family; an amended claim, written after development, passed. Appendix C gives the verdict, the
erratum, and the failure modes it found.

Questions. Q1: does R3 (F111 ) leave a lower or higher identity-replacement gain than C∗ , at the
fixed pair? Q2: what do the priority phase, the residual targeting, and the pacing contribute, alone
and together? Q3: do the results hold against X7-SC, X5-AG/v2, the defence-aware sealed family
H4, welfare attacks, and coalitions, and does R4 repair the reserve collapse? Q4: does the first test’s
comparison of B3 and R3 under D2+H3 replicate on fresh markets? Q1 and Q4 are confirmatory;
Q2 and Q3 are descriptive.

Lock and draw. The protocol file binds the code commit, the rules, families, tuned files, man-
ifest, decision rules, predictions, budget, and calibration multipliers. It was signed and tagged
protocol-v2, stamped with OpenTimestamps, and pushed with its upgraded proof when the Bit-
coin tip was at block 974379. The earliest attestation of the proof is at block A = 974307; the
draw block is H = A + 144 = 974451, read once both explorers reported at least six blocks above
it with the same hash. Had the push come at or after H, the draw would have been kept and its
results labelled nonconfirmatory. We assume that the chain both explorers served at that read is
the accepted chain and that no miner chose the block at H to influence this evaluation; a later
reorganisation deeper than six blocks is not handled and would be reported.

Fresh markets. The block hash seeds a generator of market instances around the headline market:
fixed scale, noise, budget ratio, audit cost, and cost-quality association, with provider ceilings, costs,
capacities, and the top job value drawn per instance (Appendix F). Six instances and 200 evaluation
seeds per market were drawn from it. The claims concern this neighbourhood, not markets in
general.

