   189	\section{Honest identity churn}\label{sec:churn}
   190	% BEGIN-EV2
   191	In the registered markets every honest provider keeps one identity for the whole episode, so after the start the newest identities are mostly the attacker's. This section asks what remains of R3's advantage when honest providers also replace identities. It is an exploratory follow-up after the report and changes no registered decision. The last part of the section reports a replication on new markets that was planned and timestamped before they were drawn. In both the follow-up and the replication, seed-bootstrap intervals condition on the six markets, market-level $t$ intervals use variation across those markets, and both condition on the selected search outputs and omit search uncertainty.
   192	
   193	\paragraph{Design.} Each of the nine honest opponents retires its identity and re-enters under a fresh one with probability $p$ in each round, at $p = \const{0.01}$, \const{0.02}, \const{0.03}, and \const{0.05} (mean identity lifetimes of \const{100}, \const{50}, about \const{33}, and \const{20} rounds; \site{ch_reps1}, \site{ch_reps2}, \site{ch_reps3}, and \site{ch_reps5} honest replacements per episode). For B3, R3, and C$^*$ on the six fresh markets at $\rho=\const{0.2}$ we reran the registered D2+H3 search, evaluation, and doubling check, each against the rule's frozen truthful reference played under the same churn; the reference menus were not regenerated. The rates are illustrative, not calibrated to any market. The plan and four expectations for $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} were written before any churn search, after a timing check on one market had shown the old attack's profit under R3 rising with $p$; the rates \const{0.02} and \const{0.03} were added by amendment after those results were seen, with four further expectations written before any run at these rates. Churn keeps capacity and quality fixed, but it also resets an honest provider's history and lets it leave a false ban, so it does not isolate age information.
   194	
   195	\paragraph{Gains and contrasts across the churn rates.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn} and Figure~\ref{fig:churn} give the pooled gains and contrasts. The gain against R3 rises from \site{ch_r3_0} without churn to \site{ch_r3_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$ ([\site{ch_r3_5_lo}, \site{ch_r3_5_hi}]), while the gains against C$^*$ and B3 change much less (from \site{ch_c_0} to \site{ch_c_5}, and from \site{ch_b_0} to \site{ch_b_5}). The C$^*$ minus R3 difference falls from \site{ch_d_0} to \site{ch_d_1}, \site{ch_d_2}, \site{ch_d_3}, and \site{ch_d_5} as $p$ rises; the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{ch_q_0} without churn and \site{ch_q_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$, and then falls to \site{ch_q_2}, \site{ch_q_3}, and \site{ch_q_5}. With the frozen searches the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 difference changes sign between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}; with the doubled searches it is already \site{ch_d_1_d2} at \const{0.01}. The pooled B3 minus R3 gap changes sign between \const{0.03} and \const{0.05} under both search versions: at $p = \const{0.05}$ the attack earns more against R3 than against the rule without audit priority. At $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is more than three times the materiality margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$, in C$^*$'s favour.
   196	
   197	\paragraph{How firm the reversals are.} The intervals in Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn} hold the six markets fixed. Treating the markets as a sample, Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-t} gives exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences for every rate and search version: every one includes zero except B3 minus R3 at $p = \const{0.05}$ with the doubled searches. The churn reversals therefore have narrow seed-bootstrap intervals on these six markets, intervals that hold the selected policies fixed and omit search uncertainty, and they are not established beyond these markets. Market by market: at $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_5_neg} of the six markets and B3 minus R3 on \site{ch_q_5_neg} with the frozen searches, and on \site{ch_d_5_dneg} and \site{ch_q_5_dneg} with the doubled searches (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{ch_d_5_d}; B3 minus R3: \site{ch_q_5_d}); the frozen B3 value at this rate depends on one flagged cell. At $p = \const{0.03}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_3_neg} of six markets with the frozen searches and on \site{ch_d_3_dneg} with the doubled ones, while B3 minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_q_3_neg} and \site{ch_q_3_dneg}, so its positive pooled value there does not hold market by market. At $p = \const{0.02}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on only \site{ch_d_2_neg} markets: one market dominates the pooled value, and with the frozen searches it sets the sign. At $p = \const{0.01}$ the C$^*$ minus R3 difference is \site{ch_d_1_x} with the frozen searches and \site{ch_d_1_d} with the doubled ones, so the comparison with C$^*$ is not resolved at \const{0.01} or \const{0.02}, while the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{ch_q_1_x} and \site{ch_q_1_d}. \site{ch_inc} of the \site{ch_inc_n} doubling checks at $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} and \site{ch_inc_mid} of the \site{ch_inc_mid_n} at \const{0.02} and \const{0.03} are flagged incomplete; the largest flagged change is \site{ch_flag_max}. Reselection within each rule's frozen reference menu under churn gives a nonzero training-set reference-score change in \site{ch_resel} of the \site{ch_resel_n} cells, with a largest absolute change of about \site{ch_resel_max}; this diagnostic does not bound the change in held-out gain, and the menus themselves were not regenerated.
   198	
   199	\paragraph{Change from no churn (exploratory, post hoc).} The intervals above concern the ordering at each churn rate. A separate question is whether churn worsens R3's relative position in the same market. Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-delta} pairs each market's contrast with its own no-churn value, per search version: at $p = \const{0.03}$ and \const{0.05}, both contrasts are lower than without churn on every sampled market under both search versions. This supports a deterioration of R3's measured relative performance on these markets, while the ordering under churn beyond them remains uncertain. These paired comparisons were computed after an external review, are unadjusted, rest on six markets, and omit search uncertainty.
   200	
   201	\paragraph{Where R3's audits go.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit} shows how each rule spends its audits under its selected attack. Under R3 the share of the attacker's jobs that is audited falls from \site{ch_fa_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_fa_2}, \site{ch_fa_3}, and \site{ch_fa_5}, while under C$^*$ it moves from \site{ch_fa_c1} to \site{ch_fa_c5} and under B3 it is \site{ch_fa_b5}. With the doubled searches R3's value at $p = \const{0.01}$ is \site{ch_fa_1d} (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}). At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{ch_yj_5} of the jobs and receive \site{ch_ya_5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{ch_est_5}. With the frozen searches, on two or three of the six markets, depending on the rate, R3's selected attack never replaces its identity (with the doubled searches, none at $p = \const{0.01}$; Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); there the attacker does not hide among the newcomers but stays old, and, once past its own priority window, is audited only from what the priority phase and newest-first targeting leave. Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} does not cover such an attacker, since it bounds the audits of a replacement identity. Where the selected attack does replace (\site{ch_rep_n} markets at $p = \const{0.05}$), R3 audits between \site{ch_rep_lo} and \site{ch_rep_hi} of its jobs with the frozen searches and between \site{ch_rep_lo_d} and \site{ch_rep_hi_d} with the doubled ones, and the attacker is uniquely the newest job-holding identity (by first offer, ties excluded) in only \site{ch_new_lo} to \site{ch_new_hi} of the rounds in which it holds jobs (frozen); where it stays old (the same \site{ch_stay_n} markets under both search versions), R3 audits between \site{ch_stay_lo} and \site{ch_stay_hi} of its jobs with the frozen searches and between \site{ch_stay_lo_d} and \site{ch_stay_hi_d} with the doubled ones. The measured share falls at each of the four sampled rates; four rates cannot show whether the decline is smooth or has a threshold. Audit share alone does not determine gain: R3's audit rate on the attacker falls below B3's between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, while the pooled B3 minus R3 gap stays positive through \const{0.03}. The diagnostics describe the setting; they do not isolate the cause, because churn also resets histories, ends bans, and changes the selected attacks. Consistent with them, the rise in R3's gain comes mainly from the attack's profit: in raw profit units it rises by \site{ch_pa_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$, while the reference's profit changes by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]).
   202	
   203	\paragraph{Efficiency.} Under its selected attack, R3's gross efficiency falls from \site{ch_eff_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_eff_5} at \const{0.05} (\site{ch_eff_5d} with the doubled searches); C$^*$'s is \site{ch_eff_c5} at \const{0.05} and changes little across the rates (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); at \const{0.05} B3's is \site{ch_eff_b5} with the frozen and \site{ch_eff_b5d} with the doubled searches, and across the rates it shows no trend in $p$; it moves between rates on single markets where the selected attack differs. On \site{ch_eff_minm} at $p = \const{0.05}$, R3's efficiency under attack is \site{ch_eff_min}, below the registered threshold of \const{0.90}; this is an exploratory point estimate, not a registered failure. The registered efficiency condition is a no-churn result.
   204	
   205	\paragraph{Expectations.} Scored on point estimates under both search versions, all eight expectations are met: $g_{\const{0.05}}(\mathrm{R3})$ above its no-churn value (``\site{ch_e1}''); C$^*$ minus R3 at \const{0.05} below its no-churn value (``\site{ch_e2}''); B3 minus R3 positive on all six markets at \const{0.01} (``\site{ch_e3}''); the audited share of honest replacements in their first ten rounds higher under R3 than under C$^*$ and B3 at \const{0.05}, \site{ch_sh_r3} against \site{ch_sh_c} and \site{ch_sh_b} (``\site{ch_e4}''); that share under R3 falling across the four rates (``\site{ch_e5}''); C$^*$ minus R3 lower at \const{0.03} than at \const{0.02} (``\site{ch_e6}''); $g_{\const{0.02}}(\mathrm{R3})$ below the midpoint of the no-churn and \const{0.05} values (``\site{ch_e7}''); and B3 minus R3 positive on at least five of six markets at \const{0.02} (``\site{ch_e8}''). The expectations were directional and carried little risk once the timing check had been seen.
   206	
   207	\paragraph{Prospective-exploratory replication on new markets.} After two external reviews of this section, we planned a replication before any of its outcomes existed: the plan, its five expectations, the analysis code, and its tests were committed, timestamped with OpenTimestamps, and published, and six new markets (rep0 to rep5) were then drawn from a later Bitcoin block by the second test's market generator. The replication runs R3, C$^*$, B3, and the runner-up comparator C-thr(\const{12}) at $p = \const{0}$, \const{0.03}, and \const{0.05}, with D2+H3 attacks searched on the new markets, each rule's selected no-churn reference candidate replayed under the same churn, the registered evaluation and doubling check in every cell, and continued search of up to three rounds, under the same churn, for every cell whose doubling check was flagged. Of the \const{72} cells, \site{rp_cont} were flagged and continued (\site{rp_cont0} at $p = \const{0}$, \site{rp_cont3} at \const{0.03}, and \site{rp_cont5} at \const{0.05}; \site{rp_cont_r3} of them R3's), and every continued cell passed the continuation check within three rounds; passing that check is not a claim of convergence. The primary analysis uses each cell's frozen winner unless its doubling check was flagged, and the continued winner otherwise; the all-frozen set is a sensitivity analysis. The support criterion for a contrast at a rate is descriptive: its mean paired change from the same market's no-churn value is below zero, and the change is negative on at least five of the six markets; it is not a significance test. Its material companion requires a mean change at or below the registered Q1 margin of $-\const{0.05}$; both are reported for both winner sets. The fourth expectation carried a stated prospective probability of \const{0.5}. For the fifth, R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs is the number of the attacker's audited jobs over its served jobs, each summed over a market's \const{200} evaluation seeds; a market where the attacker serves no job at either rate counts as not lower. Seed-bootstrap intervals are \const{95}\% paired percentile intervals from \const{9999} resamples (seed \const{0}), resampling seeds within each market; market $t$ intervals are \const{95}\% intervals with \const{5} degrees of freedom. We call the replication prospective-exploratory: its plan was fixed and stamped before its markets existed, but it is not part of either registered test and changes no registered decision.
   208	
   209	\paragraph{Replication results.} Table~\ref{tab:replication} gives the paired changes and the absolute contrasts; Appendix~\ref{sec:app-rep} gives them per market, with the attack and reference terms, the efficiency of every rule, and R3's audit allocation. With the primary winners all five expectations are met: the support criterion holds for C$^*$ minus R3 at $p = \const{0.05}$ (``\site{rp_e1}''), for B3 minus R3 at \const{0.05} (``\site{rp_e2}''), for both at \const{0.03} (``\site{rp_e3}''), and for the runner-up minus R3 at \const{0.05} (``\site{rp_e4}''), and R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs is lower at \const{0.05} than without churn on at least five of six markets (``\site{rp_e5}''). The mean paired changes are \site{rp_d5} ($d$), \site{rp_q5} ($q$), and \site{rp_u5} ($u$) at $p = \const{0.05}$, and \site{rp_d3}, \site{rp_q3}, and \site{rp_u3} at \const{0.03}. The material companion holds for \site{rp_mat} of the six contrast and rate pairs with the primary winners and for \site{rpf_mat} with the all-frozen winners; the support criterion holds for \site{rp_sup} and \site{rpf_sup}. These means rest mainly on two markets: at $p = \const{0.05}$ with the primary winners, $d$ changes by \site{rp_d5_t0} and \site{rp_d5_t1} on rep0 and rep1, and by between \site{rp_d5_rlo} and \site{rp_d5_rhi} on the other four; with the primary winners the other contrasts and the rate \const{0.03} show the same pattern (Table~\ref{tab:rep-markets}). The material companion therefore describes the mean change, not a typical market. With the primary winners, the only market $t$ interval of a mean change that excludes zero is that of B3 minus R3 at \const{0.05} ([\site{rp_q5_lo}, \site{rp_q5_hi}]); with the all-frozen winners the three intervals at \const{0.05} exclude zero and none at \const{0.03} does. With the all-frozen winners, the first, second, fourth, and fifth expectations are met (``\site{rpf_e1}'', ``\site{rpf_e2}'', ``\site{rpf_e4}'', ``\site{rpf_e5}''), and the third is ``\site{rpf_e3}'': C$^*$ minus R3 at \const{0.03} is lower than without churn on only \site{rpf_d3_k} of six markets, so it fails the support criterion while meeting the material companion. Continued search moved both the no-churn baselines and the churn cells: the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 difference with the primary winners minus that with the all-frozen winners is \site{rp_sd0} without churn, \site{rp_sd3} at $p = \const{0.03}$, and \site{rp_sd5} at \const{0.05}. With the all-frozen winners the mean paired changes at \const{0.05} are \site{rpf_d5} ($d$), \site{rpf_q5} ($q$), and \site{rpf_u5} ($u$), further from zero than with the primary winners: at this rate the better-searched winner set gives the weaker changes. The paired changes therefore carry the search uncertainty of the baselines as well as that of the churn cells. As planned, the failed expectation is reported without a causal reading.
   210	
   211	\paragraph{Ordering and audits on the new markets.} At $p = \const{0.05}$ with the primary winners, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{rp_ad5} and negative on \site{rp_ad5_n} of six markets, the runner-up minus R3 is \site{rp_au5} (negative on \site{rp_au5_n}), and B3 minus R3 is \site{rp_aq5}, negative on \site{rp_aq5_n} markets (\site{rpf_aq5_n} with the all-frozen winners). The sign reversals of the earlier follow-up thus recur on the new markets in point estimates, but with the primary winners all three market $t$ intervals of these absolute contrasts include zero (Table~\ref{tab:replication}); the pooled seed-bootstrap interval of B3 minus R3, which holds the six markets fixed, is [\site{rp_aq5_blo}, \site{rp_aq5_bhi}] (Table~\ref{tab:rep-absolute}). At $p = \const{0.03}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{rp_ad3} ([\site{rp_ad3_lo}, \site{rp_ad3_hi}]) and negative on only \site{rp_ad3_n} of six markets (\site{rpf_ad3_n} with the all-frozen winners), against all six under both search versions in the earlier follow-up; the new markets do not repeat that result. R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs falls from between \site{rp_fa0_lo} and \site{rp_fa0_hi} without churn to between \site{rp_fa5_lo} and \site{rp_fa5_hi} at $p = \const{0.05}$, and is lower on \site{rp_fa_below} of six markets. At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{rp_yj5} of the jobs and receive \site{rp_ya5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{rp_est5} (equal-weight means over the six markets; Table~\ref{tab:rep-alloc}). At $p = \const{0.03}$ the primary allocation covers only \site{rp_al3_n} of six markets: the run code computed diagnostics for continued R3 winners only at $p = \const{0}$ and \const{0.05}, so rep2, rep3, and rep4, whose R3 winners at \const{0.03} come from continued search, have none; this is a gap against the plan, which asks for the allocation at every rate. With the primary winners, R3's selected attack at $p = \const{0.05}$ replaces its identity on \site{rp_x5} of six markets and keeps one identity on the others (\site{rpf_x5} with the all-frozen winners). Under its selected attack at \const{0.05}, R3's mean gross efficiency is \site{rp_eff5} (lowest market \site{rp_eff5_min}) and C$^*$'s is \site{rp_effc5} (Table~\ref{tab:rep-terms}).
   212	
   213	\paragraph{Scope.} The earlier follow-up ran one verified-history comparator under churn; the replication adds the runner-up, and on the new markets the deterioration under its descriptive criterion is not specific to C$^*$. Every claim is restricted to the generator's market neighbourhood, the four rules, the D2+H3 family, illustrative churn rates, and the prescribed finite searches; neither the seed-bootstrap nor the market $t$ intervals cover search uncertainty. Within this model and at the tested rates, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ does not extend past the two lowest churn rates of the earlier follow-up, and its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not to \const{0.05}. On the new markets the pooled point estimates agree, but at \const{0.03} the comparison with C$^*$ is mixed across markets.
