   422	\paragraph{Result.} The original claim failed against H2: R3 passed the rule clause, but neither audited baseline met either baseline criterion (Table~\ref{tab:verdict1}). The amended claim passed: under D2+H3 the mean gains were \evone{0.570} for B2, \evone{0.353} for B3, and \evone{0.071} for R3. These are normalised excess focal profits, not losses of honest surplus; and the first report's sentence that R3 matched B3 and B2 under H2 ``within noise'' was unsupported, since no equivalence test was run (published erratum).
   423	
   424	\paragraph{The language-model attacker.} H1, a language model playing the focal provider at $T = \evone{100}$, was exploratory. Its R3 result is inconclusive by the registered rule, because R3's audit selection cannot be expressed in the description vocabulary given to the actor; Amendment 4 raised H1's cost cap once after 11 of its 12 cells had been seen.
   425	
   426	\paragraph{Failure modes.} Exploratory robustness cells on reused development seeds found three: at high audit cost ($\rho = \evone{1}$) R3's terminal reserve consumes the whole allowance and R3 audits nothing early; high noise lowers every audited rule's efficiency; and coalitions of entrants overload the newcomer priority. The full timeline and the robustness table are in the supplement; the record and its claims are unchanged.
