8    The second test: results
The registered estimates and decisions below come from the report that the tagged code wrote;
the addendum beside it (Appendix D) adds labels and disclosures. Table 26 reports the registered
descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic. Tables 24, 25, 28, 13, and 14 report exploratory analyses
of existing records, and Tables 19 and 20 the separate registered-configuration stability check; none
of these changes a registered estimate or decision. Gains are normalised attacker gains; intervals
are those of Table 2. Some descriptive fixed-reference contrasts on fresh1 (12 interval entries in the
report’s per-market Q2 rows) have numerically extreme studentized intervals: the reference-profit
contrast there is above rounding scale on only 3 of 200 seeds (the others are at most 1.2e-13 in
absolute value), and 506 of the 9,999 frozen resamples (5.1 percent) omit all three, so their standard
error is of rounding size and their bootstrap-t statistics are huge. We retain these frozen outputs,
list them in the audit file, and draw no inferential conclusion from them; no other interval in the


                                                    10
report has a bound beyond 1000 in absolute value.

Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1). Pooled over the six fresh markets, the
prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C∗ higher than
against R3 by 0.021 under D2+H3 and by 0.024 under X7-SC; the mean gains are 0.086 against C∗
and 0.065 against R3 under D2+H3, and 0.091 and 0.067 under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject
d = 0 (calibrated input p′ = 0.0004; Holm-adjusted p = 0.0008 for each). Both intervals lie inside
the registered margin of ±0.05: the categories are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” (D2+H3) and
“equivalent, direction R3 lower” (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is “efficiency condition
met”. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected
comparator; the margin of ±0.05 is half the first test’s comparative margin, not an estimated
threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference
changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from −0.038 on fresh3 to 0.053
on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search
winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table 25, exploratory) changes
fresh3’s difference from −0.038 to 0.015, and the pooled differences to 0.030 under D2+H3 and
0.024 under X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. Two exploratory follow-ups after the report bear
on this comparison. Continued searches of 19 registered cells, 14 whose doubling check was flagged
and 5 unflagged X7-SC cells with held-out shortfalls (up to three rounds each), all passed the
incremental-improvement check within three rounds, which bounds neither global convergence nor
the remaining search error; they included no churn cells; with their final winners substituted, C∗
minus R3 is 0.028 under D2+H3 and B3 minus R3 is 0.169, and the largest change of a market
difference is 0.054 (fresh3, where the difference becomes positive). The development runner-up
comparator, C-thr(12), evaluated on the same six markets without churn, gives the attacker a pooled
D2+H3 gain lower than against R3 (runner-up minus R3: −0.010, [−0.011, −0.009]; “equivalent,
direction comparator lower”, a label from nominal percentile intervals with the registered margin,
not the registered calibration and Holm procedure); R3 has the lower gain on 4 of six markets, and
fresh5 (−0.059) contributes most of the frozen pooled difference. Table 29 gives all four family and
search versions: every market-level t interval includes zero, R3 has the lower gain on four or five of
six markets, and C∗ minus runner-up is positive in all four; 3 of the 6 D2+H3 runner-up doubling
checks and 1 of 6 under X7-SC are flagged incomplete. The registered direction holds for C∗ , not
for verified-history targeting in general. The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six
fixed markets. Treating the six markets as a sample of the neighbourhood (exploratory), a t interval
over the per-market differences is [−0.013, 0.054] under D2+H3, with 5 of six markets positive, and
[0.005, 0.044] under X7-SC; with the doubled-search winners it is [0.014, 0.045] under D2+H3, with
6 positive. With each rule’s strongest training-selected family the interval is [0.009, 0.052], with
6 of six markets positive. The direction is therefore fairly stable across these analyses, while the
size is not: every upper end is near the margin, so the equivalence label is the most fragile part;
beyond these six markets the sign is not established either, since the frozen interval contains zero
and the searches share inputs; on these markets the registered direction tests reject zero for the
prescribed searches, and another comparator would be a different comparison. The frozen D2+H3
interval contains zero and crosses the margin, so this analysis does not establish the category for
the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside the margin, but that family
mostly repeats D2+H3 (below). In relative terms, under D2+H3, the attacker’s pooled mean gain
against C∗ exceeds that against R3 by a fraction of 0.319. The two Q1 families are close to one
test: X7-SC’s search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner, and in 10 of the 12 Q1 cells it
selected that winner, with identical evaluated gains; only fresh3 against C∗ and fresh4 against R3


