differ. With the headline market, the same holds in 12 of 14 market–rule cells, which is why most
of prediction Q3-a’s differences are exactly zero. With each rule’s strongest training-selected family
instead (Table 15), the pooled difference is 0.031, and 0.070 on fresh1, where X5-AG/v2 is C∗ ’s
strongest family. C∗ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget
runner-up left a lower development gain than C∗ (Section 4); a comparator selected on attacker
gain could change the direction label. The two rules differ more in whom they burden than in the
attacker’s gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market
(Table 26), an honest provider replaces its identity at round 50: R3 audits 1.000 and 0.917 of the
replacement’s served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C∗ audits 0.428 and 0.161 (its lowest
bin; 0.231 in the next), and B3 about 0.250. Under R3 the replacement earns less per round (relative
to C∗ , a fraction of −0.044; the registered diagnostic stores bin means, so no interval is available)
and loses more receipts to refunds paid to buyers on false audit failures; R3 directs more of the fixed
audit capacity to this provider, which does not show higher total audit spending. Both decisions are
about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test
uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on
the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section 7). The paired descriptive differences in Table 27 are
small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction;
the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting d with the same normalisation into the
attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table 24, exploratory) shows that almost all of
it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C∗ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.

Repeating the comparison on six new markets (Q4). The registered B3–R3 gain-and-
efficiency comparison under D2+H3 passes on 3 of the six markets (Table 4), and all three passes
(fresh1, fresh3, fresh4) are about the prescribed search procedure. On every market the estimated
B3 minus R3 gain difference is positive and R3 keeps its efficiency condition; where the comparison
fails, the difference is smaller than the registered threshold, not reversed: 0.041 on fresh0, 0.057 on
fresh2, and 0.027 on fresh5, against 0.469 on fresh1. R3’s efficiency on fresh2, the lowest, is 0.985.
The decisions on fresh0, fresh1, fresh3, and fresh4 are about the prescribed search procedure (fresh0
and fresh3 through their references; addendum). The registered prediction of at least five passing
markets failed. Q4 does not test the complete amended claim of the first test. Table 28 (exploratory)
