advantage when honest providers also replace identities. It is an exploratory follow-up after the
report and changes no registered decision. The last part of the section reports a replication on new
markets that was planned and timestamped before they were drawn. In both the follow-up and
the replication, seed-bootstrap intervals condition on the six markets, market-level t intervals use
variation across those markets, and both condition on the selected search outputs and omit search
uncertainty.

Design. Each of the nine honest opponents retires its identity and re-enters under a fresh one
with probability p in each round, at p = 0.01, 0.02, 0.03, and 0.05 (mean identity lifetimes of 100,
50, about 33, and 20 rounds; 17.901, 35.777, 53.790, and 89.582 honest replacements per episode).
For B3, R3, and C∗ on the six fresh markets at ρ = 0.2 we reran the registered D2+H3 search,
evaluation, and doubling check, each against the rule’s frozen truthful reference played under the
same churn; the reference menus were not regenerated. The rates are illustrative, not calibrated to
any market. The plan and four expectations for p = 0.01 and 0.05 were written before any churn
search, after a timing check on one market had shown the old attack’s profit under R3 rising with
p; the rates 0.02 and 0.03 were added by amendment after those results were seen, with four further
expectations written before any run at these rates. Churn keeps capacity and quality fixed, but
it also resets an honest provider’s history and lets it leave a false ban, so it does not isolate age
information.

Gains and contrasts across the churn rates. Table 6 and Figure 1 give the pooled gains and
contrasts. The gain against R3 rises from 0.065 without churn to 0.272 at p = 0.05 ([0.268, 0.275]),
while the gains against C∗ and B3 change much less (from 0.086 to 0.098, and from 0.238 to 0.220).
The C∗ minus R3 difference falls from 0.021 to 0.001, −0.020, −0.090, and −0.173 as p rises; the
B3 minus R3 gap is 0.173 without churn and 0.175 at p = 0.01, and then falls to 0.119, 0.068, and


                                                  14
−0.052. With the frozen searches the pooled C∗ minus R3 difference changes sign between p = 0.01
and 0.02; with the doubled searches it is already −0.004 at 0.01. The pooled B3 minus R3 gap
changes sign between 0.03 and 0.05 under both search versions: at p = 0.05 the attack earns more
against R3 than against the rule without audit priority. At p = 0.05, C∗ minus R3 is more than
three times the materiality margin of ±0.05, in C∗ ’s favour.

How firm the reversals are. The intervals in Table 6 hold the six markets fixed. Treating
the markets as a sample, Table 7 gives exploratory t intervals over the six market differences for
every rate and search version: every one includes zero except B3 minus R3 at p = 0.05 with the
doubled searches. The churn reversals therefore have narrow seed-bootstrap intervals on these six
markets, intervals that hold the selected policies fixed and omit search uncertainty, and they are
not established beyond these markets. Market by market: at p = 0.05 C∗ minus R3 is negative
on 6 of the six markets and B3 minus R3 on 6 with the frozen searches, and on 5 and 5 with the
doubled searches (C∗ minus R3: −0.169; B3 minus R3: −0.033); the frozen B3 value at this rate
depends on one flagged cell. At p = 0.03, C∗ minus R3 is negative on 6 of six markets with the
frozen searches and on 6 with the doubled ones, while B3 minus R3 is negative on 3 and 3, so its
positive pooled value there does not hold market by market. At p = 0.02, C∗ minus R3 is negative
on only 2 markets: one market dominates the pooled value, and with the frozen searches it sets the
sign. At p = 0.01 the C∗ minus R3 difference is 0.001 with the frozen searches and −0.004 with
the doubled ones, so the comparison with C∗ is not resolved at 0.01 or 0.02, while the B3 minus R3
gap is 0.175 and 0.153. 13 of the 36 doubling checks at p = 0.01 and 0.05 and 8 of the 36 at 0.02
and 0.03 are flagged incomplete; the largest flagged change is 0.088. Reselection within each rule’s
frozen reference menu under churn gives a nonzero training-set reference-score change in 32 of the
72 cells, with a largest absolute change of about 0.009; this diagnostic does not bound the change
in held-out gain, and the menus themselves were not regenerated.

Change from no churn (exploratory, post hoc). The intervals above concern the ordering
at each churn rate. A separate question is whether churn worsens R3’s relative position in the
same market. Table 8 pairs each market’s contrast with its own no-churn value, per search version:
at p = 0.03 and 0.05, both contrasts are lower than without churn on every sampled market
under both search versions. This supports a deterioration of R3’s measured relative performance
on these markets, while the ordering under churn beyond them remains uncertain. These paired
comparisons were computed after an external review, are unadjusted, rest on six markets, and omit
search uncertainty.

Where R3’s audits go. Table 9 shows how each rule spends its audits under its selected attack.
Under R3 the share of the attacker’s jobs that is audited falls from 0.366 at p = 0.01 to 0.190, 0.093,
and 0.042, while under C∗ it moves from 0.363 to 0.292 and under B3 it is 0.251. With the doubled
searches R3’s value at p = 0.01 is 0.505 (Table 9). At p = 0.05, honest identities younger than
ten rounds serve 0.391 of the jobs and receive 0.823 of R3’s audits, and established honest jobs are
audited at 0.078. With the frozen searches, on two or three of the six markets, depending on the
rate, R3’s selected attack never replaces its identity (with the doubled searches, none at p = 0.01;
Table 9); there the attacker does not hide among the newcomers but stays old, and, once past its
own priority window, is audited only from what the priority phase and newest-first targeting leave.
Lemma 2 does not cover such an attacker, since it bounds the audits of a replacement identity.
Where the selected attack does replace (3 markets at p = 0.05), R3 audits between 0.041 and 0.095
of its jobs with the frozen searches and between 0.037 and 0.051 with the doubled ones, and the


                                                  15
attacker is uniquely the newest job-holding identity (by first offer, ties excluded) in only 0.008 to
0.033 of the rounds in which it holds jobs (frozen); where it stays old (the same 3 markets under both
search versions), R3 audits between 0.012 and 0.015 of its jobs with the frozen searches and between
0.012 and 0.015 with the doubled ones. The measured share falls at each of the four sampled rates;
four rates cannot show whether the decline is smooth or has a threshold. Audit share alone does not
determine gain: R3’s audit rate on the attacker falls below B3’s between p = 0.01 and 0.02, while
the pooled B3 minus R3 gap stays positive through 0.03. The diagnostics describe the setting; they
do not isolate the cause, because churn also resets histories, ends bans, and changes the selected
attacks. Consistent with them, the rise in R3’s gain comes mainly from the attack’s profit: in raw
profit units it rises by 113.101 at p = 0.05, while the reference’s profit changes by −0.146 ([−0.393,
0.098]).

Efficiency. Under its selected attack, R3’s gross efficiency falls from 0.989 at p = 0.01 to 0.967
at 0.05 (0.949 with the doubled searches); C∗ ’s is 0.993 at 0.05 and changes little across the rates
(Table 9); at 0.05 B3’s is 0.940 with the frozen and 0.959 with the doubled searches, and across the
rates it shows no trend in p; it moves between rates on single markets where the selected attack
differs. On fresh2 at p = 0.05, R3’s efficiency under attack is 0.893, below the registered threshold
of 0.90; this is an exploratory point estimate, not a registered failure. The registered efficiency
condition is a no-churn result.

Expectations. Scored on point estimates under both search versions, all eight expectations are
met: g0.05 (R3) above its no-churn value (“met”); C∗ minus R3 at 0.05 below its no-churn value
(“met”); B3 minus R3 positive on all six markets at 0.01 (“met”); the audited share of honest
replacements in their first ten rounds higher under R3 than under C∗ and B3 at 0.05, 0.571 against
0.356 and 0.250 (“met”); that share under R3 falling across the four rates (“met”); C∗ minus R3
lower at 0.03 than at 0.02 (“met”); g0.02 (R3) below the midpoint of the no-churn and 0.05 values
(“met”); and B3 minus R3 positive on at least five of six markets at 0.02 (“met”). The expectations
were directional and carried little risk once the timing check had been seen.

Prospective-exploratory replication on new markets. After two external reviews of this
section, we planned a replication before any of its outcomes existed: the plan, its five expectations,
the analysis code, and its tests were committed, timestamped with OpenTimestamps, and published,
and six new markets (rep0 to rep5) were then drawn from a later Bitcoin block by the second test’s
market generator. The replication runs R3, C∗ , B3, and the runner-up comparator C-thr(12) at
p = 0, 0.03, and 0.05, with D2+H3 attacks searched on the new markets, each rule’s selected no-
churn reference candidate replayed under the same churn, the registered evaluation and doubling
check in every cell, and continued search of up to three rounds, under the same churn, for every cell
whose doubling check was flagged. Of the 72 cells, 20 were flagged and continued (6 at p = 0, 6 at
0.03, and 8 at 0.05; 8 of them R3’s), and every continued cell passed the continuation check within
three rounds; passing that check is not a claim of convergence. The primary analysis uses each
cell’s frozen winner unless its doubling check was flagged, and the continued winner otherwise; the
all-frozen set is a sensitivity analysis. The support criterion for a contrast at a rate is descriptive:
its mean paired change from the same market’s no-churn value is below zero, and the change is
negative on at least five of the six markets; it is not a significance test. Its material companion
requires a mean change at or below the registered Q1 margin of −0.05; both are reported for both
winner sets. The fourth expectation carried a stated prospective probability of 0.5. For the fifth,
R3’s audited share of the attacker’s jobs is the number of the attacker’s audited jobs over its served


