11     What the results show and what they do not
What the two tests support together. At the fixed parameters and without honest churn,
the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled
market, with R3’s registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule’s
frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities
beyond the exploratory D2-family comparison of Section 9, which holds the parameters and search
budgets fixed and compares two separately searched winners. The registered comparison does not
establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the
comparison with C∗ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do
not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At ρ = 0.2, pacing slightly raises
the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at ρ = 1, the terminal reserve that pacing adds
exhausts the allowance and causes R3’s collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.

What they do not support. The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross ef-
ficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference
against R3 and C∗ ; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Exploratory
mixtures expose policies omitted by the prescribed W1 search that have substantially lower empir-
ical efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; their population profitability remains unestablished,
and the favourable W1 result for R3 and C∗ is not a lower bound on efficiency. Robustness to
the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot
bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The
fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the
frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision
unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.

Limits. The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither
equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition 1 establishes a
profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every
studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets
come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. R3’s
registered advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities.
With honest churn the pooled advantage over C∗ is unresolved at the two lowest rates and negative
from 0.03, and the pooled advantage over B3 changes sign at the highest (Section 10); that follow-up
is exploratory, uses illustrative rates and frozen references, runs one verified-history comparator, and
leaves the comparison with C∗ unresolved at the lowest rate. Its prospective-exploratory replication
on new markets adds the runner-up comparator but tests only two nonzero churn rates beside the
no-churn baseline and a descriptive criterion, and its market-level intervals mostly include zero.
The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower
development gain.


12     Conclusion
For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against
honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next
allocation, free replacement removes the ban’s continuation cost. At the fixed parameters and
without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for


                                                   21
B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three
of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-
history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets
under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; with the frozen searches the difference varies in
sign across markets (under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule’s strongest family,
it is positive on all six), and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood.
At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about
0.840 against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C∗ .
Against R3 and C∗ , the registered W1 searches selected policies with zero estimated gain and gross
efficiency near 0.997; exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower
efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies.
In an exploratory follow-up, most of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed
searches disappears when attacker replacement is disabled: under the D2 family, neither pooled
mean gap (B3 minus R3, or C∗ minus R3) is distinguishable from zero using exploratory market-
level t intervals. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest
newcomers, the share of the attacker’s jobs it audits falls from 0.366 to 0.042 between p = 0.01
and 0.05, and its measured advantage reverses: at p = 0.05, C∗ minus R3 is −0.173 and B3 minus
R3 is −0.052 (−0.033 with the doubled searches; the frozen value includes one search-flagged cell
with a large improvement under doubling). Against C∗ , the one verified-history comparator run
under churn in that follow-up, the comparison is unresolved at p = 0.01 and 0.02, and at 0.03 C∗
minus R3 is negative on every market under both search versions; against uniform audits its pooled
advantage lasts through p = 0.03 but not to 0.05. A prospective-exploratory replication on six
new markets, fixed and timestamped before they were drawn, finds with its primary winners the
same deterioration from no churn against C∗ , the runner-up comparator, and uniform audits under
its descriptive criterion; at p = 0.05 C∗ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are negative on 6 and 6 of
the new markets, but at 0.03 C∗ minus R3 is negative on only 4, the mean changes rest mainly
on two markets, and the market-level intervals mostly include zero. The registered Q1 and Q4
inferences use calibration admitted on development fixtures; the follow-up intervals are exploratory:
seed-bootstrap intervals condition on the six sampled markets, while market-level t intervals treat
those markets as a sample; both hold the selected attacks and references fixed and omit search
uncertainty, and the doubled searches are sensitivity analyses. The record of both tests, including
the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with
the paper.

