These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-
level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, has
the probability-weighted mean of its components’ gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain
constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing
evaluation records. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation
records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of 0.932 for R3 and 0.921 for C∗ at non-negative
estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3’s mixture (0.945) meets the registered training
constraint, and C∗ ’s does so at the smaller weight 47.65 percent, with efficiency 0.9224 against
0.997 under the registered W1 search (Tables 13 and 14). Weights and companions were chosen on
the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided 95 percent bound, so these rows show
that the prescribed W1 search did not consider episode-level mixtures that reach lower empirical
efficiency; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished.
The mixture efficiencies stay above 0.90; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate.
