C    The first test (October 2026)
Claims. The original claim was locked and timestamped on 17 October 2026, before the rule was
implemented. At β = 0.05, the median position, and on 40 fresh seeds, its rule clause required, for
each confirmatory family, a one-sided 95% lower bound of R3’s gross efficiency of at least 0.90, a
one-sided 95% upper bound of its gain of at most 0.30, and audit cost within budget on every seed.
Its baseline clause required, for each of B2 and B3, a confirmatory family in which either the paired
inflation-caused efficiency-loss difference exceeded 0.05 or the baseline’s gain exceeded 1.0, at the
98.75% level. Amendment 1, registered after the baselines’ development results showed that the
baseline clause was unlikely to pass, added an amended claim on the public identity-replacement
family D2+H3: the same absolute part as the rule clause (gross-efficiency lower bound at least 0.90,
gain upper bound at most 0.30, budget compliance) and paired gain reductions of at least 0.10
against B2 and B3 at the 98.75% level, plus the H2 rule clause. After a development test killed the
first rule, Amendment 3 registered one successor, R3, before its implementation.

Sealing. The sealed family H2 was written in a separate session from a pinned copy of the repos-
itory, and only its hash was committed. Before unsealing, at the author’s request, the custodian
disclosed that H2 contains D2; that H2 does not exit or re-enter was stated in the public specification
before the lock.

Result. The original claim failed against H2: R3 passed the rule clause, but neither audited
baseline met either baseline criterion (Table 34). The amended claim passed: under D2+H3 the
mean gains were 0.570 for B2, 0.353 for B3, and 0.071 for R3. These are normalised excess focal
profits, not losses of honest surplus; and the first report’s sentence that R3 matched B3 and B2
under H2 “within noise” was unsupported, since no equivalence test was run (published erratum).

The language-model attacker. H1, a language model playing the focal provider at T = 100,
was exploratory. Its R3 result is inconclusive by the registered rule, because R3’s audit selection
cannot be expressed in the description vocabulary given to the actor; Amendment 4 raised H1’s
cost cap once after 11 of its 12 cells had been seen.

                                                  37
Failure modes. Exploratory robustness cells on reused development seeds found three: at high
audit cost (ρ = 1) R3’s terminal reserve consumes the whole allowance and R3 audits nothing early;
high noise lowers every audited rule’s efficiency; and coalitions of entrants overload the newcomer
priority. The full timeline and the robustness table are in the supplement; the record and its claims
are unchanged.


D     Registration detail
Calibration. A multiplier κ replaces each nominal tail αe by καe , so κ < 1 widens the intervals.
It was selected from {1, 1/2, 1/4} on fixtures built from frozen development attacks and references
on the headline market and dev0 (pools of 20000 seeds per market; Q1 in six equal strata), using
10000 selection replications, and admitted on 20000 independent validation replications if every
endpoint’s Clopper–Pearson upper bound on its miss rate, at confidence 1 − 0.01/m with m = 8
endpoints for Q1 and m = 36 for Q4 (the two-market branch evaluates 24 Q4 endpoints and
keeps the denominator 36, which is conservative), was at most its allocated tail. The fixtures hold
the policies and references fixed, so admission does not cover search or reference selection, and
it does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule distributions. Q1’s categories
follow the logic of equivalence testing and Q4’s market test is an intersection-union test [4]; the
calibration follows the reporting of simulation studies of statistical methods [23]. Conditional on
valid calibrated component p-values, Holm controls the Q1 direction family and the six Q4 joint
gain-and-efficiency decisions separately; fixture admission does not establish that validity on the
fresh-market distributions. Q1’s reported intervals use calibrated Bonferroni tails, and its efficiency
condition uses a separate nominal one-sided 97.5 percent bound. The report stores the calibrated
two-sided Q1 value that enters Holm and, for Q4, the raw one-sided bootstrap p-values of the two
components and the calibrated market value max(p′gain , p′eff ) with p′ = min(1, p/κ); Tables 3 and 4
print these with their Holm-adjusted values, and an audit script recomputes these values from the
records and reproduces each Q1 category and direction rejection and each Q4 decision.

Development. All choices before the draw were development, on the headline market and the
development instance dev0 (branch two20k): a benchmark priced every cell; the comparator was
selected and the tuned variants were tuned; the defenders were frozen at a signed commit; a fresh
session of another model (gpt-6.1-sol), started by the author, wrote H4 against the frozen defenders;
after a machine restart the executor resumed the same session at the author’s instruction, without
reading its log or bundle, committed the bundle in the author’s separate clone, and only its hash
entered the protocol; a coverage check on frozen development attacks admitted calibration multi-
pliers κ = 0.5 for Q1 and κ = 0.25 for Q4; and the predictions were written and hashed. Budget
caps were 12 USD for development and 40 USD for evaluation, within a written authorisation of 52
USD. The evaluation cap forced three registered cuts: the tuned variants, H4, and the D2 extras
run on the headline market only.

Deviations from the protocol. Two arose during the evaluation. First, the runner recalibrates
each fresh market where it runs, and the evaluation ran on Linux x86 machines, while the registered
instance file holds the calibration computed on the author’s arm64 machine. In each fresh market,
between 1 and 6 provider parameters differ between the two in the last binary digit (one unit in
the last place, a relative difference near 10−16 ); the headline market is unaffected. The runner’s
verification compares configuration hashes exactly and refuses these records. We kept them: a
container on the evaluation platform reproduces each run’s configuration hash, every differing value


                                                  38
is within one unit in the last place of the registered one, and verification passes on every other check
when given the reproduced configuration. The check is a script in the artifact. It establishes that the
configurations match, not that the decisions are unchanged, since small input differences can change
discrete simulation branches; the re-evaluation below holds the selected attacks and references fixed
and does not repeat search or reference selection under the registered configurations. We therefore
re-evaluated the frozen policies of every fresh-market cell that Q1 and Q4 use on the registration
platform with the registered configurations, against the frozen references: per-seed gains change in
every cell, cell means move by less than one hundredth, and every Q1 and Q4 decision is unchanged
(docs/revision/deviation/stability.py; Table 19). The report’s addendum lists the hashes,
states the interval labels, and traces search-flag dependencies through each cell’s references, which
qualifies three further decisions as about the prescribed search procedure. Second, the fresh-market
runs cost about ten percent more than estimated, and the author raised the evaluation cap from 40
to 43 USD and the written authorisation from 52 to 55 USD so that every registered cell could run;
no analysis changed. The exploratory follow-ups after the report ran under a separate follow-up cap
of 72 USD (program limit 123 USD, the author’s approval of 12 November 2026). Recorded spend:
identity-replacement follow-up 1.65 USD; churn at p = 0.01 and 0.05, 4.99 USD; churn at 0.02 and
0.03, at most 7.70 USD; runner-up comparator, at most 3.14 USD; continued searches, 2.76 USD
for a first attempt that lost its results to a killed worker and 3.40 USD for the rerun (the two upper
bounds are ceiling estimates; the others are billed amounts or balance differences).


E     Exact but costly checks
The model’s audit already reveals delivered quality exactly, at a cost charged to the operator’s bud-
get. An execution proof such as FlashProve checks a different object, that a committed computation
ran, and does not remove delivery noise. It fits the model’s audit cost only if the operator funds
both proving and verification; provider-funded proving would need separate incentive accounting.
Either way, which jobs to check and how to sanction remain the questions; if the cost of a proof fell
close to that of the computation itself, the case for sampled audits would weaken. This discussion
is outside the registered tests and changes none of their claims.


F     Parameters and definitions
First test and headline market: providers i = 1, . . . , 10 have qimax = (0.60, 0.65, . . . , 1.00, 1.00), bi =
(0.40, 0.38, . . . , 0.22), ai = 0.02, ki = 6, [vmin , vmax ] = [0.6, 1.4], σ = 0.05, β = 0.05, ρ = 0.2. Fresh
markets of the second test: N , J, T , σ, β, ρ, and vmin as at the headline; q max ten sorted draws from
U [0.55, 1.0]; bi falling linearly in the quality rank between bhi ∼ U [0.34, 0.46] and blo ∼ U [0.18, 0.26]
with noise U [−0.02, 0.02]; ai ∼ U [0, 0.05]; ki uniform in {4, . . . , 8}; vmax ∼ U [1.2, 1.6]. The focal
provider is at the median position. The first-best surplus S ∗ of a seed is the sum over rounds of the
maximum-weight assignment of jobs to capacity slots at full effort.


G     Proofs
Theorem 1 (conditional renewal bound). Consider immediate-replacement plans whose play decom-
poses into independent, identically distributed cycles of an established spell followed by a recovery.
Conditional on survival, each established round has expected profit rE and failure probability qE > 0;
the recovery has duration D ≥ L with ED < ∞, profit RF , and NR replacements, with integrable
rewards and charges. The long-run profit per round is Π̄ = (rE /qE + ERF − e ENR )/(1/qE + ED).

