codex
The replication arithmetic and table checks passed. I found no unresolved cross-references. I will recommend submission after minor corrections. The main changes are to remove the author’s name, shorten the main text, and keep headline claims within the scope of the evidence.

codex
VERDICT: submit-after-minor

CORRECTIONS:

1. **main.tex:14; PDF page 1 — Remove the author’s name.** The supplied PDF identifies the author outside Availability. Hiding Availability links does not fix this submission blocker. Check the final anonymous PDF, including metadata, against the [WEIS anonymity rule](https://weis2026.econinfosec.org/).

2. **main.tex:27–40, 163, 181, 193–213, 253 — Shorten and reorganise the main text.** The central question is visible by page 2, but the contributions and results continue onto page 3. The 23-page main text gives too much space to search histories, repeated qualifications, and expectation scoring. Move these details to the appendix. Keep Q1/Q4, a compact factorial/welfare panel, the churn allocation evidence, and Table 10 in the body. Lead with the economic tradeoff between checking newcomers and maintaining coverage of established providers. Move the detailed FlashProve discussion out of the opening.

3. **main.tex:21 — Correct the abstract’s scope.** “In two registered simulation tests … we compare … with … C*” implies that both tests included C*; only the second did. State this explicitly. Also qualify the approximately 0.840 welfare result as a **headline-market** result. Keep the prescribed-search and search-flag qualifications close to the registered findings.

4. **main.tex:22, 38 — Limit the replacement claim.** “Locate R3’s advantage” and “It sits in the attacker’s identity replacement” exceed the narrower evidence described at lines 181 and 370. Use: “Within the prescribed D2 searches, disabling replacement removes most of the estimated B3–R3 gap.” The separately searched winners can differ in every knob; this does not identify the optimal value of replacement.

5. **main.tex:195, 213, 249, 253; Tables 7–10 — Distinguish point estimates from established ordering.** Replace unqualified statements that an advantage “lasts,” “does not extend,” or “reverses” with statements about **pooled point estimates on the sampled markets**. Table 10 correctly separates deterioration from absolute ordering: with primary winners, all three absolute-contrast market intervals at \(p=0.05\) include zero. Keep this distinction in every summary. The five-versus-four expectation count, six-versus-five support count, and six material companions agree with the JSON inputs and plan.

6. **Figure 1; Tables 9, 30, 31 — Improve the evidence display.** Figure 1 foregrounds smooth pooled curves from the earlier follow-up without showing market variation. Add a compact display of the replication’s per-market paired changes and market intervals, with primary and frozen winners identified. Split or rotate the wide tables: Table 9 and the interval rows in Tables 30–31 use very small text despite substantial unused page space.

7. **main.tex:105, 38, 253, 256 — Remove stale descriptions.** Theorem 1 bounds long-run **profit**, not an “audit rate.” Align “to be released” and “will be released” with Availability’s statement that the release is already public. I found no unresolved cross-references; the source-number check and replication-table consistency check passed.

TOP-3:
1. Remove the author’s name and verify the final anonymous PDF.
2. Cut repeated detail and put the economic question and main evidence within the first two pages.
3. Tighten headline scope and show replication heterogeneity beside pooled results.

CONFIDENCE: 0.91
